title: $:/plugins/nikorion/font-manager/language/en-GB/readme
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

A web font manager for TiddlyWiki. Drop a font file (`.woff2`, `.woff`, `.ttf`, `.otf`) onto the wiki: it is turned into a self-contained stylesheet tiddler (`$:/fonts/<Name>`, content type (MIME type) `text/css`, tagged ''$:/tags/Stylesheet'') holding a `@font-face` rule with the font embedded in base64 — no network request, works offline and in single-file wikis.

! Usage

Drag a font file onto the wiki and import it from the usual import screen. Once imported, the typeface is available to any CSS in the wiki. Reference it by name with `font-family`, e.g. in a stylesheet tiddler:

```css
.tc-tiddler-title { font-family: "Lobster"; }
```

Or inline in wikitext:

```
@@font-family:"Caveat Brush";Some fancy text@@
```

Imported fonts are always stored as `text/css` tiddlers. TiddlyWiki does not strictly require it for a stylesheet, but it is the right choice: with the default wikitext type, TiddlyWiki would parse the very long base64 data as wikitext (slow, and a `//` sequence, which base64 can contain, would be read as italic markup and corrupt the font). If you create a font tiddler by hand, give it the `text/css` type too. The plugin itself ships no font.

! WOFF2 conversion

`.ttf` and `.otf` files are converted to WOFF2 while they are imported (typically 40 to 80 % smaller). The encoder is only loaded the first time a font is converted. Turn this off in ''Settings'' > ''Font Manager''. `.woff` and `.woff2` files are kept as they are.

! Saving to disk (Node.js wiki folders)

With a wiki folder served by Node.js, TiddlyWiki writes an imported font next to your other tiddlers, as a flattened file such as `$__fonts_Lobster.css`. To keep them together in a sub-folder, add this line at the top of a tiddler titled `$:/config/FileSystemPaths` (one filter per line, the first match wins):

```
[prefix[$:/fonts/]search-replace:g:regexp[/|\\],[_]addprefix[system/fonts/]]
```

Fonts then go to `tiddlers/system/fonts/`. If you already have a `$:/config/FileSystemPaths` tiddler, add the line above the existing ones. This is a TiddlyWiki setting, so a plugin cannot do it for you; single-file wikis are not concerned.
